Answer: Put a breakpoint on a line by double-clicking on the left margin (or right-click and toggle breakpoint), then right-click the program and choose Debug As > Java Application. Eclipse switches to the Debug perspective, and the control stops at the breakpoint. Press F6 to execute one line at a time (step over). The Variables view on the side shows the current values, such as i. 3. Trace the execution of the following do-while loop and give its output.

```java
int i = 1 is created. The control enters the do block, prints i and increases it. Then the condition is checked: if true, the control goes back to the do block; if false, the loop ends. This repeats while i is 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5. After printing 5, i becomes 6, the condition 6 <= 5 is false, so the control leaves the loop and i stays 6.
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
int i = 1;
do {
System.out.println(i);
i++;
} while (i <= 5);
}
}
```

Output: 1 2 3 4 5

Answer: The loop repeats while m is not equal to 0, and the three steps of Euclid's algorithm are written inside it.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
System.out.print("Enter two numbers: ");
int n = sc.nextInt();
int m = sc.nextInt();
while (m != 0) {
int r = n % m;
n = m;
m = r;
}
System.out.println("GCD = " + n);
}
}
```

Sample input and output: Enter two numbers: 8 16 GCD = 8

Answer: Create a separate class (here GCD) with a method that performs the task and returns the value. In the main class, take the two numbers from the user, create an object of the GCD class, call the method, and store the returned value in a variable.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
class GCD {
int findGcd(int n, int m) {
while (m != 0) {
int r = n % m;
n = m;
m = r;
}
return n;
}
}
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
System.out.print("Enter two numbers: ");
int a = sc.nextInt();
int b = sc.nextInt();
GCD g = new GCD();
int result = g.findGcd(a, b);
System.out.println("GCD = " + result);
}
}
```

Sample input and output: Enter two numbers: 8 16 GCD = 8
